# Fixture pattern rules for the end-to-end harness.
#
# NOT a shipped rule pack: docs/DESIGN.md §13 step 3 owns those.  These exist so
# step 1 can prove the plumbing runs from parse through finding through report.
# They carry no context-require / context-deny, because evaluating the context
# window is the SAST engine's job at step 3 and this repository does not have a
# second implementation of it.

id: SAST-PY-EVAL-01
format-version: 1
title: Use of eval() on request-derived data
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-95
owasp: A03:2021
pattern: \beval\s*\(
dialect: ere
files: *.py
tags: static
tags: quick
remediation: Do not pass request-derived data to eval().
  Parse literals with ast.literal_eval(), or dispatch through an explicit
  allowlist of permitted operations.
references: CWE-95

id: SAST-SEC-AWS_SECRET-01
title: Hardcoded AWS secret access key
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-798
owasp: A07:2021
pattern: (aws_secret_access_key|AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY)[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*"[A-Za-z0-9/+=]{40}"
dialect: ere
files: *.py
tags: static
tags: quick
severity-floor: high
remediation: Remove the key from source and rotate it immediately; a committed key must be
  treated as compromised even if the commit was reverted, because it remains in git history.
references: CWE-798

id: SAST-PY-YAML_LOAD-01
title: yaml.load() without a safe loader
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-502
owasp: A08:2021
pattern: \byaml\.load\s*\(
dialect: ere
files: *.py
tags: static
severity-floor: medium
remediation: Use yaml.safe_load(), or pass Loader=yaml.SafeLoader explicitly.
references: CWE-502
